<!-- Problem -->
<section id="the-problem" class="section alt">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2>Growth should be a good problem. It usually isn't.</h2>
    <p>You've built something people actually use. Now traffic is climbing, data is growing, and cracks are appearing where you least expect them. What worked at launch won't work at scale, and the cost of getting it wrong is measured in outages, lost customers, and frantic all-hands incidents at 2 a.m.</p>
    <p>Most architecture books teach patterns. This one teaches you how to think: about availability, about risk, about the organizational forces that make scaling hard. Then it gives you concrete techniques to act on it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- What's Inside -->
<section id="the-book" class="section">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2>A practical framework for systems that have to work.</h2>

    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      "Don't bet against your business. Build as if being successful at scale is a foregone conclusion."
      <cite>Colin Bodell, VP Engineering, Shopify Plus</cite>
    </blockquote>

    <p class="subsection-heading">What the book covers</p>
    <ul class="benefits-list">
      <li>How scaling changes service availability, and how to architect around those changes before they become incidents</li>
      <li>Service-based application design for high availability, including fault isolation and graceful degradation</li>
      <li>Single Team Owned Service Architecture (STOSA): scaling your organization alongside your system</li>
      <li>How to identify, measure, and reduce risk in distributed systems, with a free downloadable Risk Matrix Template</li>
      <li>Cloud architecture patterns that let you take full advantage of elastic, scalable infrastructure</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

